---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeSubnet from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeSubnet.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ip-range-to-cidr">
  <DevEncodeSubnet client:load mode="range-cidr" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why one range becomes several blocks</h2>
    <p>
      A CIDR block has two constraints: its size must be a power of two, and it must start on an
      address that is a multiple of that size. An arbitrary IP range rarely meets both, so an exact
      cover needs a sequence of blocks that grow as the alignment improves and shrink again at the end.
    </p>
    <pre><code>10.0.0.5 – 10.0.0.16

10.0.0.5/32   1 address   (5 is odd, so only a /32 fits)
10.0.0.6/31   2 addresses (6 is even)
10.0.0.8/29   8 addresses (8 is a multiple of 8)
10.0.0.16/32  1 address   (only one left)
= 12 addresses, exactly</code></pre>
    <p>
      The algorithm is greedy and provably minimal: at each step it takes the largest block that starts
      at the current address and fits inside the rest of the range.
    </p>

    <h2>Exact, never approximate</h2>
    <p>
      Each block is fully inside the range you gave, and no address outside it is included. The output
      usually becomes a firewall rule or a security-group entry. A tool that rounded up to the nearest
      tidy block would grant access to addresses you did not intend.
    </p>
    <p>
      For fewer, larger blocks that cover more than the range, widen the range you type. That way the
      decision is yours and explicit.
    </p>

    <h2>Where this conversion is required</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>AWS security groups, NACLs and route tables accept CIDR only, not ranges.</li>
      <li>Azure NSG rules and GCP firewall rules have the same restriction.</li>
      <li>Kubernetes NetworkPolicy <code>ipBlock</code> fields.</li>
      <li>nginx <code>allow</code>/<code>deny</code> and Apache <code>Require ip</code>.</li>
      <li>BGP prefix lists and route filters.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Vendors often send their addresses as &ldquo;203.0.113.10 to 203.0.113.60&rdquo; in an email.
      This page converts that range to CIDR.
    </p>

    <h2>Sanity checks before you paste the output</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Count the addresses. The page shows the total. If it is much larger than you expected, one
        octet is probably wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        Look for a huge first block. A <code>/8</code> in the list means the range is much wider than
        intended.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check the boundaries. Ranges in documentation usually include both ends. Some tools treat the
        last address as exclusive.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use fewer rules where you can. Some platforms limit the number of rules per group, and a poorly
        aligned range can give a dozen blocks.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Aligning the range makes life easier</h2>
    <p>
      If you control the allocation, choose ranges that start on a power-of-two boundary. A range
      from <code>10.0.0.0</code> to <code>10.0.0.255</code> is one clean <code>/24</code>; the same
      256 addresses starting at <code>10.0.0.5</code> need eight separate blocks. Aligned ranges keep
      firewall configuration readable.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
